In CGAL's `Regular_triangulation_3`, vertices are accessed via `rt.finite_vertices_begin()` and `rt.finite_vertices_end()`. The position is retrieved via `v->point()`. Since this is a Regular Triangulation, the "weight" (which often relates to mass in these physics simulations) is typically stored in the weighted point. Velocity and mass are likely stored in the vertex's data or calculated from the triangulation's properties.

For the classification, a common pattern in Adhesion model implementations is to count the number of edges connected to a vertex; if the number of edges (or the length of edges) exceeds a threshold, the node type is updated.

```cpp
    std::vector<Node> nodes;
    for (auto vit = rt.finite_vertices_begin(); vit != rt.finite_vertices_end(); ++vit) {
        Node node;

        // Extract position from the weighted point
        auto p = vit->point();
        node.position = {p.x(), p.y(), p.z()};

        // In the Adhesion model, velocity and mass are typically associated 
        // with the vertex data or the point's properties.
        // Assuming the RT stores these in the vertex's associated data.
        node.velocity = vit->velocity();
        node.mass = vit->mass();

        // Classification based on edge threshold
        // We count edges connected to the vertex that are shorter than the threshold
        int edge_count = 0;
        rt.cycle_vertices_around_vertex(vit, [&](auto neighbor) {
            auto dist = std::sqrt(std::pow(p.x() - neighbor->point().x(), 2) +
                                  std::pow(p.y() - neighbor->point().y(), 2) +
                                  std::pow(p.z() - neighbor->point().z(), 2));
            if (dist < threshold) {
                edge_count++;
            }
        });

        // Assign node type based on the connectivity/threshold
        node.type = (edge_count > 0) ? 1 : 0; 

        nodes.push_back(node);
    }
    return nodes;
